WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:08.710
ROB MARTIN: Hello, and
welcome to "Solutions

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.880
for Scaling Massive Global
Games in the Cloud".

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.335
I'm Rob Martin, Chief Architect
for gaming at Google Cloud.

00:00:17.335 --> 00:00:19.210
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: And
I'm Sebastian Weigand,

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.925
a planet scale systems
architect on Rob's team.

00:00:22.925 --> 00:00:24.300
ROB MARTIN: And
today we're going

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.890
to be talking a lot
about global games.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:32.030
First, we're going to define
what we mean by a global game.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.850
Next, we're going to define
all of the components that

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:38.600
are used to power a
global game in the cloud.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.270
Then we're going to do a
demo, and finally, we'll

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.270
close out with some thoughts
on a toolkit for building

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:48.160
your own global
game in the cloud.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.630
So let's start by talking
about global games.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:55.470
Gaming is the world's most
popular form of entertainment,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:59.180
with 2.7 billion
players globally,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.230
and that number is
expected to increase

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.910
by another half-billion by 2023.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:09.870
Let's talk about an example
of a truly global game.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:14.910
Apex Legends is a great
example of a truly global game.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.600
Apex Legends launched
exclusively on GCP

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.850
with our partner, Multiplay.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.280
Within the first eight
hours after launch,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.150
over a million players
had played the game.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.610
Within the first three
days, that number 10xed

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:33.540
to 10 million players, and by
the end of the first month,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.930
over 50 million players had
joined the fun in Apex Legends.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:42.590
So let's talk about
concurrency and scaling.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:47.000
A CCU is defined as
one concurrent player.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.120
Peak CCU is the maximum
number of players

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.300
during the day that will be
playing your game all at one

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.390
time.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.520
You can see many points of peak
CCU illustrated in the diagrams

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.110
to the right.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.240
As players join
your game, you'll

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.820
need to scale up infrastructure
to meet that demand.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.680
So in some sense, as
more CCUs join your game,

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.220
you'll need to scale up more
CPUs in the cloud to power

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.710
the multiplayer experiences.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.750
Let's take a look at a
few different examples

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.620
of peak CCUs.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.100
So the first example
is a premium launch

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.170
where the game is launched
perhaps at retail,

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.760
there's a very large peak
CCU spike on the first day,

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.160
and then you settle into
a predictable pattern

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.240
in the following days.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.910
Or in the second
example, you might

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:38.270
have a mobile launch where
you have a limited beta,

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.400
and then over time,
user acquisition

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.850
opens up to add more and
more concurrent players.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:45.710
What we're seeing
more and more of today

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:49.460
with today's free-to-play
games like Apex Legends

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:54.440
that incorporate content
drops, seasons, and live events

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.080
is you're seeing peaks
happening throughout the game's

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.410
lifecycle, so in today's
world, it's very important

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.920
to be able to scale to
these unpredictable demand

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.220
peaks that are generated by
these new types of content

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.120
releases.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.880
And so we've talked about
concurrency and scaling.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.220
Now let's talk about geography.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.330
So using an example game with
a million concurrent players,

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.160
those players are
going to be distributed

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.840
all around the Earth.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.370
Using this example of a
typical AAA PC console game,

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.520
we would expect about 40%
of those concurrent players

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.100
to be located in North America.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.990
We'd see about 30% of those
players in Europe, about 10%

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:41.100
in South America, and then
the remaining 20% of players

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.950
distributed across the
Middle East and Asia.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.460
Now, because latency
matters in gaming,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.160
you're going to need to
deploy your compute resources

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.440
all around the Earth
to get them as close as

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.910
possible to your players.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.960
So as Seb and I
prepared for this demo,

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.870
we thought to
ourselves, would it

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.890
be possible to scale up all
of the compute infrastructure

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.440
required to run a
global game for one

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.320
million concurrent
players within the space

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.690
of this 25-minute demo?

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.203
What do you think, Seb?

00:04:14.203 --> 00:04:15.870
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND:
With the right tools,

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:18.709
anything is possible.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:19.450
All right, Rob.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:20.680
Let's dive in.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.330
I've created these
clusters already,

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:24.700
and I've installed
Agones onto each of them.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.520
Right now, these are
essentially empty clusters

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.530
running only a few nodes each.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.800
I've got one cluster in the
US, another in the Netherlands,

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.960
and a third in Japan.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.030
Because our cloud console
aggregates information

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:39.880
across clusters, I can see that
I don't have anything running

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.130
in these clusters yet
besides the pods needed

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:43.660
to run Agones itself.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:46.600
I've coded up a game server in
Go, which simulates a battle

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.160
royale-style game.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.440
It records the events
which typically take place

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.990
into BigQuery, our
data warehouse.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:54.880
It also creates
player information,

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.310
such as match rankings,
and writes those

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.120
into Spanner, our horizontally
scalable relational database.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.920
To deploy this
server worldwide, we

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.740
define it using an Agones
game server fleet spec

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:09.960
combined with a Google Cloud
Game Servers deployment.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.960
The fleet specification looks
just like any other Kubernetes

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:13.830
resource.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.740
Agones provides an abstraction
layer on top of Kubernetes

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.840
to understand the
concept of a game server

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.350
or a collection of game
servers called a fleet.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.000
It does this
because game servers

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.055
aren't your typical
stateless workload.

00:05:26.055 --> 00:05:28.680
You don't want your game to get
killed in the middle of a match

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.650
and scheduled on another
node, for example.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.195
Likewise, Kubernetes needs
to know more information

00:05:34.195 --> 00:05:35.820
about the state that
the game server is

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.880
in, such as total player
count, to handle functions

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.200
like scaling properly.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.320
Agones takes care of
things like this for us

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.880
so that we don't have to.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.590
Let's take a look at
our current fleets

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.470
on each of the GKE clusters.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:53.730
As you can see, we don't
have any fleets deployed.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:56.850
Let's go ahead and create
a ton of game servers.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.430
To do this, I'm
going to ask GCGS

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:03.390
to apply a new rollout using
the configs I've specified.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.060
What it'll do is connect
to every GKE cluster

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.060
in each of the realms and
create the Agones fleets

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.190
based upon our fleet spec.

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.490
In short, we'll go from
no pods to thousands

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.808
across multiple clusters
in multiple continents.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.180
Here you can see the
number of servers

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.910
that we want, the number
which we are booting up,

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:26.830
and the number which are ready.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:29.200
The allocated column
would update once players

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:31.330
start connecting to them.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:35.050
Rob, you asked about scaling up
enough resources for a million

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:36.670
concurrent players.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.650
Deploying thousands
of these servers

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:42.880
across 10,000 CPU-optimized
cores ought to do the trick.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.470
Let's check those GKE clusters.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:52.620
Remember, we only had
one node in each zone.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.380
Let's see what it's up to now
that the Cluster Autoscaler has

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:56.610
seen our requests spike.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:02.420
This is great.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:04.040
The Cluster
Autoscaler has already

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.890
begun adding additional
nodes to these clusters based

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.214
upon the resources
that we've requested.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:14.840
OK, more and more game
servers are coming up.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.870
Agones and GKE are
working together

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:21.290
to spin up additional virtual
machines to handle this load.

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:23.920
This will take just a couple
of minutes to scale up.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.440
Let's check back
in on these later.

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:27.950
Back to you, Rob.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:28.950
ROB MARTIN: Thanks, Seb.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.280
It'll be interesting
to watch that scale up.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:32.820
And while that's
happening, let's

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:35.640
talk a little bit about the
components that are required

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.260
to power a global game.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.500
So first, you need
a global network

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.930
to connect with your players.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:47.100
Next, you need high-performance
compute to run your simulations

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.890
and your multiplayer matches.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.050
Then you need a global
database instance

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:56.110
to track player progress
throughout the game.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:57.970
You need to monetize
your game, so you'll

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:01.690
need planet scale analytics
that can absorb all the events

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:03.640
you're going to be posting.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:06.220
And then finally, you need a
fleet orchestrator to bring it

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:09.250
all together, and to scale
this infrastructure up

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:13.030
as players join your game, and
to scale it down as players

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:15.680
move on to other activities.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.740
Let's take a look at each
one of these components

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:18.365
in more detail.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.700
Let's start with
our global network.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:26.210
The first thing that you'll
notice on this diagram

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:29.940
is the 24 regions
distributed around the globe.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.390
These are represented
as colored circles

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:33.919
and are the
geographies where you

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:38.220
can run your compute resources
to power your matches.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:43.049
The next thing you'll notice is
our 144 edge points of presence

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:47.250
that are located as close
as possible to your players.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.350
Now, the unique and
differentiated thing

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.200
on this diagram is the
fiber optic network

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:56.070
that ties our edge points of
presence and all of our regions

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:57.630
together.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:01.020
Only Google Cloud offers
a global private fiber

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.110
optic network out of the box.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.060
Next let's talk
about compute, which

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.460
you'll need to power
your multiplayer matches.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:12.000
So we know that there are
many different types of games,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.950
from a casual,
mobile title that you

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:18.840
play on your phone with friends
to a powerful and high-fidelity

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:22.590
multiplayer battle royale
match that you play on your PC

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:24.240
or console.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:27.510
At Google Cloud, we've
created a spectrum of compute

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:30.850
that you can tailor to the
specific needs of your game.

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:32.790
So for that casual
title, you can

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:36.960
run on our cost-optimized VMs,
and for that high-fidelity

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.240
battle royal title,
you can run it

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.110
on our compute-optimized VMs,
which run at high clock speed

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:49.130
and we built specifically to run
demanding workloads like games.

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:52.190
Next, let's talk about
a scalable database.

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:54.380
As players progress
through your game,

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:57.080
you'll need a way to capture
updates like rankings

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.360
and updates to inventory.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:02.950
The way that a database for a
global game is typically built

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:06.670
is by utilizing a commodity
database like MySQL

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:10.520
and running tens or even
hundreds of instances to power

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.110
a global game.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.600
This technique is
not globally scalable

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:18.020
and, from an ops
perspective, is not awesome.

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.900
At Google, we offer an
alternative approach

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:25.640
with Spanner, a horizontally
scalable relational database.

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.790
We offer the capability to
run a single instance that

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:32.900
can serve all of your players
globally without shards.

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:34.388
Now that's awesome.

00:10:34.388 --> 00:10:36.680
So we've talked about a lot
of different infrastructure

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:40.045
components that will be required
to power your global game.

00:10:40.045 --> 00:10:42.170
It's going to be important
that you can effectively

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:46.910
monetize, particularly in
today's free-to-play world.

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:50.840
BigQuery offers a globally
scalable data warehouse

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:54.200
that can scale from launch
to the world's biggest games

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:57.000
with zero ops for your team.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.490
Candy Crush is probably
the best example

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.890
of scaling running on BigQuery.

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:04.800
Candy Crush ingests
over 70 billion events

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.625
per day seamlessly, and
Google manages all the ops.

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.270
So to tie it all
together, you'll

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:16.750
need to scale these resources
up and down throughout the day

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:20.860
as players join the game and
move on to other activities.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:24.220
To manage the scaling of all
of these resources deployed

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:28.900
globally, we built Google Cloud
Game Servers, a global control

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:31.360
plane for running your game.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:33.400
Google Cloud Game
Servers is built

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:36.080
on top of open
source technology,

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:38.770
including a Agones
and Kubernetes, which

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:40.750
allows you to run
your infrastructure

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:45.220
components wherever you need
them, in GCP, on other clouds,

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:46.720
or on bare metal.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:50.270
Let's take a closer look.

00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:53.810
In this diagram, we're
showing the Game Servers API,

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:56.240
which is your global
API and control

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:58.910
plane for managing your game.

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:01.340
On the left, we have
one Kubernetes cluster

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:05.300
that's running in Google
Cloud platform on GKE.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:07.190
On the right hand
of the diagram,

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:08.840
we have another
Kubernetes cluster

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:11.690
that's running on
premise, on bare metal.

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:14.090
Both clusters are
being centrally managed

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:16.790
by Google Cloud Game
Servers, and all

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:19.250
of the operational
events and log events

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:22.650
are being captured centrally.

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:26.640
Agones and GCGS offer
a new cloud-native way

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:28.420
to build your game.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:30.870
Let's talk about the roadmap
for building your game

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:33.180
with this new technology.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.220
First, you
containerize your game

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.360
and implement the open
source Agones SDK.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.000
Next, you register
each of your clusters

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:45.810
with the Game Services
API control plane.

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.450
Then you begin
organizing your clusters

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:52.440
into groups by geography
that we call realms.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:55.680
Next, you define configs
for things like autoscaling

00:12:55.680 --> 00:13:00.030
policies, and finally, you test
and rollout your game server

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:03.150
binary to your global
fleet of game servers

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:06.970
running on Kubernetes.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:08.780
So let's check in
on our scaling.

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:10.572
Seb, how do you think
things are looking?

00:13:10.572 --> 00:13:12.280
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND:
Pretty good from here.

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:14.830
Let's take a look.

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:16.570
OK, Rob, let's take
a look and see how

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:18.280
our servers are coming along.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.550
So far, we've got 4,200
servers running in the US,

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:24.250
a little over 3,300
running in the EU,

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:26.630
and 500 running in Japan.

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:29.020
This is truly a global
fleet, more than enough

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:31.630
to support one million
concurrent players

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:35.740
as each server can handle
at least 150 players.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:37.660
It took under eight
minutes to scale up

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:40.210
all the compute needed
for a massive deployment

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:41.710
all around the world.

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:45.280
This really demonstrates just
how easy and powerful managed

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:46.540
Agones really is.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:58.985
Now let's take a
look at the database.

00:13:58.985 --> 00:14:01.110
Here we can see the number
of nodes we're currently

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:03.810
using, along with the CPU
utilization and number

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:05.580
of concurrent requests.

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:08.040
Wow, that's a lot of
requests, and this number

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.860
should keep rising as more and
more game servers come online.

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:15.520
We're running about
80,000 queries

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:17.740
per second, which is
about what you would see

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:20.210
for a very large global game.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.960
Should we need to handle more,
rather than deal with database

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:26.530
sharding, we simply increase
the node count in Spanner,

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.160
and it handles the rest.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:31.690
You got to love the separation
of compute and storage.

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:34.150
Now let's check
BigQuery, our gaming data

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:35.650
warehouse where
we've been ingesting

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:37.840
massive quantities
of telemetry events

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:39.000
since we started our fleet.

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:47.690
So far, this table is
just about 100 gigabytes,

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:49.940
with over 800 million rows.

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:54.850
Excellent.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:58.450
But let's figure out the number
of events processed per second.

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:01.060
Even though there are thousands
of game servers inserting data

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:04.330
right now, let's ask BigQuery
to take the number of events

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:07.150
between the last two minutes
and one minute and then

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:08.110
divide by 60.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:12.970
What I love about
this is we can keep

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:16.300
inserting more and more data
and let our scalable databases

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:17.800
handle the rest.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.290
When your cloud can scale
to ingest data like this,

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:23.470
your team can focus more on
creating really great games

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:25.885
and less on managing
your infrastructure.

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:35.310
Wow, over 800,000 rows
per second, not bad.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.270
So Rob, in less
than 10 minutes, we

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:44.560
scaled up close to 10,000 cores
of compute on three continents,

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:46.900
wrote over 80,000
inserts per second

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:51.010
to a global database instance,
and ingested over 800 million

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:53.290
rows to our data warehouse.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:56.080
Talk about scalability.

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:59.680
ROB MARTIN: Wow, those are
some impressive numbers, Seb.

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:03.010
We were able to scale all of
the infrastructure required

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:07.880
for a million concurrent
players in about 10 minutes.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:12.200
To recap, let's take a look at
each component in this toolbox

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:16.640
for building massive global
games on Google Cloud.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:19.970
Our premium network
offers a global fiber

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:23.750
optic network that extends your
reach to wherever your players

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:25.850
are around the globe.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:29.750
Compute-optimized VMs
offer the high clock speeds

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:33.740
required for today's
most demanding games.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:37.730
Spanner provides a global
database instance for your game

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:40.370
with no sharding.

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:43.790
BigQuery is a planet
scale data warehouse

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:47.690
that can ingest millions or
billions of events each day

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:49.440
for your game.

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:53.730
And finally, Google Cloud Game
Servers, the fleet orchestrator

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:57.510
that can scale these resources
up and down seamlessly

00:16:57.510 --> 00:17:00.540
during the day as your
players need them.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:04.000
To learn more,
join us on the web,

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:07.940
and we'd like to thank
you for joining us today.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:10.540
So Seb, that was a
pretty cool demo.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:13.480
We were able to scale
up a million concurrent

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:16.640
player fleet in
about 10 minutes.

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:18.040
So for our viewers,
what would it

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:21.585
take to scale up a fleet for
10 million concurrent players?

00:17:21.585 --> 00:17:23.710
SEBASTIAN WEIGAND: Well,
because the infrastructure

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:26.869
and technology are sort of
taking care of it for you,

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:30.790
the only thing that I could
think of would be more quota.

